/*! \page developer_backend_comm_page Device - Host Communication

\ref developer_backend_field_page (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_test_page          (Next)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

Some operations performed by the pscf_rpg program require arrays to
be explicitly copied between CPU host memory and GPU device memory. 
The two most common situations that require this are:

   - Field input and output operations that must be performed on
     the CPU host but that require access to field data that is
     allocated in GPU memory.

   - Calculations that must read or write arrays that are allocated
     on the GPU host but that are difficult to implement as parallel
     CUDA kernels, and so are instead performed on the CPU host.

The data transfer operations that are performed for these purposes
in template specializations that are used by pscf_rpg are generally 
unnecessary in corresponding specializations used by pscf_rpc, since
this program does not use GPU device and allocates all arrays in
CPU memory.

PSCF provides a pair of class templates named Pscf::HostArray and 
Pscf::ConstHostArray that each take a backend identifier class T as 
a parameter argument, and that are designed to be used in operations 
that require copying of data between the GPU device and the CPU host 
in template specializations with T=Pscf::CUT. The two templates are
declared as
\code
namespace Pscf {
   template <typename Data, typename T> class HostArray;
   template <typename Data, typename T> class ConstHostArray;
}
\endcode
where the parameter Data is the type of each array element and 
T is backend identifier class. Specializations of these templates
with T=Pscf::CUT are designed to provide a convenient syntax for
operations that copy arrays between the CPU host and CPU device,
in either direction. Specializations with T=Pscf::CPT are designed 
to use the same syntax but to not actually perform any unnecessary 
copy operations. This is done by requiring that the sequence of
function calls that is used to actual perform a "deep" copy of 
data between GPU and CPU arrays when used with specializations 
of these templates with T=Pscf::CUT instead create a "shallow" 
copy that simply makes two arrays point to the same data when 
used with specializations with T=Pscf::CPT.

The design of these two templates is based on the assumption 
that most large, long-lived arrays in PSCF are allocated on 
the GPU in specializations with T = Pscf::CUT, and are thus 
defined in template code as instances of DeviceArray<Data,T> 
or a subclass thereof.  The design is designed to allow 
operations that copy data between a long-lived DeviceArray 
and a shorter-lived HostArray or ConstHostArray, for which 
the data in the HostArray or ConstHostArray only needs to 
be accessed within the scope of a single function. 

The template HostArray is designed to be used for CPU-to-GPU 
copy operations, while ConstHostArray is designed to be used for 
GPU-to-CPU copy operations.  A HostArray defines a subscript
([]) operator that allows read and write access to elements
of the array, which is needed to allow elements to be assigned
values before they are copied to the device array.  The 
ConstHostArray instead defines a subscript operator that 
only allows read access to elements, allowing elements to be 
read after they are copied from the associated DeviceArray.

\section developer_backend_HostToDev Host-to-Device Copy

The pseudo-code shown below outlines the required usage to 
transfer data from an instance of a HostArray<Data,T> named 
hostArray to an instance of DeviceArray<Data,T> named devArray. 
We assume that the devArray container has been declared and 
allocated prior to entry to this code segment.
\code
    HostArray<Data,T> hostArray;
    hostArray.associate(devArray);

    // Assign values to all elements of hostArray

    devArray = hostArray;
    hostArray.dissociate();
\endcode
In this code snippet, the associate function, assignment 
operator, and dissociate member function perform completely
different operations for specializations with T=Pscf:CUT and 
T=Pscf:CPT.

In specializations with T=Pscf::CUT (Cuda backend):
   - The statement hostArray.associate(devArray) checks if 
     hostArray is allocated and, if it is not, allocates it with
     the same capacity as that of the devArray container that is
     passed to the associate function.
   - The statement devArray = hostArray copies all elements 
     of hostArray to devArray, by performing a CPU-to-GPU
     copy of the array.
   - The statement hostArray.dissociate() does nothing.

In specializations with T=Pscf::CPT (C++ backend):
   - The statement hostArray.associate(devArray) sets a
     pointer owned by hostArray to be a copy of the pointer
     to the array owned by devArray, thus making hostArray
     a shallow copy of devArray.
   - The statement devArray = hostArray does nothing.
   - The statement hostArray.dissociate() nullifies the 
     pointer member of hostArray that points to an 
     array, thus destroying the association between the 
     two arrays.

In specializations with T=Pscf::CPT, assignment of values to
the elements of hostArray thus automatically also assigns
values to elements of devArray, because the two containers 
temporarily refer to the same memory. In this case, the use
of a shallow copy avoids the need to actually copy any data,
while allowing the same function template to be used with 
either backend.

In specializations with T=Pscf::CPT, the pointer owned by the 
host array is null after the call to hostArray.dissociate(), 
and so does not have any access to data after this point.
To use this pattern safely in template code that must be
valid for either backend, the associate function, assignment 
operator, and dissociate function must all be called within 
the same function, and elements of hostArray may only be 
assigned values between the call to the associate member
function and the assignment (=) operator.

In specializations with T=Pscf::CUT, the HostArray owns
a separate block of CPU heap memory that can be allocated by 
the associate function. The dissociate function does not deallocate 
this memory, but the destructor function for a HostArray 
deallocates any associated memory that remains when the object 
is destroyed. If the ConstHostArray is a local variable that 
is defined within a function, as in the above example, this 
memory will thus be deallocated when the function ends and 
the host array goes out of scope.  If the ConstHostArray is 
instead a class member variable that is used in a class member 
function, then the associated memory will be allocated the 
first time the associate function is called, and can be 
re-used in multiple calls to the same or other member 
function, as long as the associated device array always has 
the same number of elements. The associate member function
throws an Exception if a ConstHostArray is already allocated
on entry but its capacity is not equal to that of the device
array that is passed as a function argument. The decision to
not use the dissociate function to deallocate host memory was 
made to allow this reuse of memory if desired. 

\section developer_backend_DevToHost Device-to-Host Copy

The pseudo-code shown below outlines the usage for transferring
data from a long-lived instance of DeviceArray<Data,T> named 
devArray to a temporary instance of ConstHostArray<Data,T> 
named hostArray:
\code
    ConstHostArray<Data,T> hostArray;
    hostArray = devArray;

    // Read elements of hostArray and use them to 
    // do any required computation.

    hostArray.dissociate();
\endcode
In this pattern, all read access to elements of hostArray must 
occur after the assignment statement "hostArray = devArray" and 
before the statement "hostArray.dissociate()". The interface of
the ConstHostArray does not allow values of individual elements
to be modified. 

The assignment and dissociate member functions of the ConstHostArray 
perform completely different operations for specializations with 
T=Pscf:CUT and T=Pscf:CPT. 

In specializations with T=Pscf::CUT (Cuda backend):
   - The statement hostArray = devArray copies all elements of
     the array from devArray to hostArray, by performing a 
     GPU-to-CPU copy.
   - The statement hostArray.dissociate() does nothing.

In specializations with T=Pscf::CPT (C++ backend):

   - The hostArray = devArray assigns a pointer member of
     hostArray the address of the array owned by devArray,
     thus making hostArray a shallow, temporary read-only 
     copy of devArray.
   - The statement hostArray.dissociate() nullifies the 
     pointer member of hostArray, thus destroying the 
     association between the two arrays.

In order for this pattern to be used safely in template code
that must work correctly with both backends, the assignment
operator, and dissociate function should all be called within 
the same function, and elements of hostArray may only accessed 
only between the calls to the assignment operator and the 
dissociate member function.

This pattern can be simplified somewhat in code in which
the hostArray is a local variable that is defined within
a function, while the array owned by the deviceArray exists
before and after the lifetime of the host array. In this
case:

  - The default constructor and assignment operator can 
    be combined into a call to a conversion operator that
    takes takes the device array as an argument. This
    effect of this conversion constructor is equivalent 
    to default construction followed by assignment.

  - The dissociate function may sometimes be omitted,
    because the destructor of ConstHostArray class 
    template will delete any memory owned by the array
    for (for T=Pscf::CUT) or release any remaining 
    association (for T=Pscf::CPT) when the function
    ends and the hostArray is destroyed. 

The most concise version of this pattern, for use with a
host array that is a local variable, is thus:
\code
    ConstHostArray<Data,T> hostArray(devArray);
    hostArray = devArray;

    // Read elements of hostArray and use them to 
    // do any required computation.

\endcode
There is, however, no harm in calling the dissociate
function explicitly in this situation, to documment the
need to release resources.

The class template ConstHostArray is designed to provide read-only access 
to array elements in order to guarantee that specializations that use 
different backends yield equivalent behavior in template code that involves 
a device-to-host copy.  If write access were allowed to elements of the 
array that received such a Device-to-Host copy, modifying elements of the 
host array would modify corresponding elements of the device array when 
used with C++ backend, because of the existence of a shallow copy, but
would have no effect on data in the device array when used with a Cuda 
backend, because in this case these two arrays uses distinct memory 
blocks allocated on different devices.  Write access is thus prohibited 
to prevent this inconsistency. 

Management of memory owned by a ConstHostArray specialization with 
T=Pscf::CUT is analogous to management of memory owned by a corresponding 
HostArray. Memory is allocated by conversion constructor or the 
assignment operator, and deallocated by the destructor, but is not 
de-allocated by the dissociate member function.

<BR>
